# sponsored-search-auction-sim — Cursor rules

## What this is
An open-source teaching/demo simulator of sponsored product search auctions
(retail media). All data is synthetic. This is an independent project, not
affiliated with Walmart or any retailer.

## Layout
- `src/auction_sim/` — library code (catalog, auction, pacing, bidders, metrics, briefs, analysis, simulate)
- `scripts/` — CLIs (`run_simulation.py`, `generate_data.py`)
- `app/` — Streamlit dashboard (thin; all logic lives in `src/`)
- `evals/` — behavior evals for optimizer/agent changes (run before merging those)
- `tests/` — unit tests for auction mechanics

## Commands
- Install: `pip install -e ".[dev]"`
- Demo run: `python scripts/run_simulation.py --n-auctions 20000 --budget 500`
- Tests: `pytest -q`
- Dashboard: `streamlit run app/streamlit_app.py`

## Conventions
- Keep `run_auction` pure-ish: no I/O, RNG passed in, catalog pre-indexed by product_id.
- New bidder strategies go in `bidders.py` and must subclass `Bidder`.
- Optimizer changes must keep `evals/test_optimizer.py` green; tighten the
  tolerance instead of deleting the eval if behavior genuinely improves.
- LLM features live in `src/auction_sim/llm/`:
  - `client.py` — provider clients (stdlib urllib only); `FakeLLMClient` for tests.
  - `tools.py` — the JSON-protocol tool loop; new agent capabilities become `Tool`s here.
  - The deterministic heuristic is always the system of record; the model
    interprets and explains, and may only act through tools.
  - Planner/narrator changes must keep `evals/test_planner.py` green.
- Synthetic data only. Never add real retailer, brand, or marketplace data.
- Keep the Streamlit app a thin view layer over `simulate.run_campaign`.
